    {{!-- scuba diving --}}

    <div class="game_name game1">


        <div class="game_imgs">
            <img class="img1" src="images/scuba_1.png" alt="">
            <h4>Underwater exploration Started!</h4>

            <img src="images/scuba_2.png" alt="">

        </div>
        <div class="game_title are1">
            <h3> scuba diving</h3>
            <p>
                <br> <b> Scuba diving</b> is a captivating underwater activity that allows individuals to explore the
                fascinating
                world beneath the ocean's surface. Here's some information about scuba diving:

                <br> <b>Equipment: </b>Scuba diving stands for "Self-Contained Underwater Breathing Apparatus." Divers
                wear a set of
                equipment that includes a mask, snorkel, fins, buoyancy control device (BCD), regulator with an air
                tank, and a wet or dry suit, depending on water temperature.

                <br> <b> Training:</b> To scuba dive safely, individuals must undergo training and certification through
                organizations like the Professional Association of Diving Instructors (PADI) or the National Association
                of Underwater Instructors (NAUI). Training covers skills such as equalizing pressure, buoyancy control,
                and emergency procedures.

                <br> <b> Breathing Underwater:</b> Scuba divers carry a tank of compressed air or other breathing gases
                that allows
                them to breathe while submerged. The regulator delivers air from the tank to the diver's mouth.

                <br> <b>Types of Diving:</b>

                <br> <b> Recreational Diving:</b> This is the most common form, where divers explore reefs, wrecks, and
                marine life
                for pleasure.
                <br> <b> Technical Diving:</b> Technical divers use specialized equipment and training to explore deeper
                depths,
                caves, or wrecks that require advanced skills and safety measures.
                Commercial Diving: Professional divers may work in underwater construction, salvage, or maintenance
                industries.
                <br> <b> Scientific Diving:</b> Scientists use diving to study marine ecosystems, gather data, and
                conduct research.
                <br> <b> Popular Dive Sites:</b> Scuba diving destinations around the world offer diverse underwater
                environments,
                such as coral reefs, kelp forests, and deep-sea trenches. Notable dive sites include the Great Barrier
                Reef in Australia, the Blue Hole in Belize, and the Red Sea in Egypt.
                <br> <b> Safety:</b> Safety is paramount in scuba diving. Divers must be aware of potential risks,
                follow safety
                protocols, and be prepared for emergencies. Buddy diving, where divers go in pairs, is a common safety
                practice.

                <br> <b> Conservation:</b> Many divers are passionate about marine conservation. They participate in
                efforts to
                protect and preserve underwater ecosystems, advocating for responsible diving practices and marine
                habitat conservation.

                <br> <b>Personal Experience:</b> Scuba diving offers a serene and immersive experience, allowing divers
                to explore
                the underwater world, experience weightlessness, and connect with nature in a unique and unforgettable
                way.

                <br> Scuba diving offers a remarkable opportunity to explore the hidden wonders of the underwater world,
                connect with nature, and experience the thrill of discovery. It's a sport that combines adventure,
                education, and a deep appreciation for the ocean's beauty and complexity.
            </p>
        </div>

    </div>

    {{!-- surfing --}}

    <div class="game_name">
        <div class="game_title">
            <h3>surfing</h3>
            <p>

                <b>Surfing</b> is a popular water sport that involves riding ocean waves on a surfboard. It's not just a
                sport
                but also a lifestyle for many enthusiasts. Here's some information about surfing:

                <br> <b>Equipment:</b> Surfers use a surfboard, typically made of foam or fiberglass, designed to float
                on
                water.
                The board features fins on the bottom for stability and control. Surfers also wear a leash attached to
                their ankle to prevent the board from drifting away.

                <br> <b>Technique:</b> The goal of surfing is to catch a wave, ride it, and perform maneuvers while
                maintaining
                balance on the board. Proper paddling, timing, and body positioning are essential for success.

                <br> <b> Types of Surfing:</b>

                <br> <b> Shortboarding:</b> Shortboards are smaller and more maneuverable, allowing surfers to perform
                tricks and
                ride steep waves.
                <br> <b> Longboarding:</b> Longboards are larger and provide stability, making them ideal for beginners
                and for
                riding smaller, slower waves.
                Big Wave Surfing: Some surfers specialize in riding enormous waves, often found at famous breaks like
                Mavericks in California or Nazaré in Portugal.
                Wave Conditions: Surfing conditions depend on factors like wave height, swell direction, tides, and
                wind. Surfers seek specific conditions to match their skill level and desired experience.

                <br> <b> Safety:</b> Safety is paramount in surfing. Surfers must be aware of rip currents, wave sets,
                and potential
                hazards in the water. Respect for fellow surfers and ocean etiquette is essential.

                <br> <b> Surf Culture:</b> Surfing has a vibrant culture that includes a love for the beach, a laid-back
                lifestyle,
                and a strong connection to nature. It's often associated with music, art, and a sense of community.

                <br> <b> History:</b> Surfing has deep historical roots in Hawaii, where it was practiced for centuries
                before
                spreading worldwide. In the 20th century, it gained global popularity, particularly in places with
                accessible coastlines and consistent waves.

                <br> <b> Competitions:</b> Surfing competitions are held at various levels, from local contests to
                international
                events like the World Surf League (WSL) Championship Tour. Surfers are judged based on factors like
                style, maneuvers, and wave selection.

                <br><b>Popular Surf Spots:</b> Surfing destinations are found worldwide, with famous spots like Pipeline
                in Hawaii,
                Uluwatu in Bali, Jeffreys Bay in South Africa, and Hossegor in France, attracting surfers from all over.

                <br> Surfing is not just a sport; it's a way of life for many who are drawn to the ocean's beauty and
                the
                thrill of riding waves. It offers a unique blend of physical activity, natural connection, and a sense
                of community that makes it a beloved pastime for people of all ages and backgrounds.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="game_imgs">

            <img class="img1" src="images/surfing_1.png" alt="">

            <h4>Let's go Wave Riders!</h4>
            <img class="img2" src="images/surfing_2.png" alt="">
        </div>

    </div>

    {{!-- flyboarding--}}


    <div class="game_name game1">
        <div class="game_imgs">
            <img class="img1" src="images/fly_1.png" alt="">
            <h4>Soar above Water!</h4>

            <img class="img2" src="images/fly_2.png" alt="">

        </div>

        <div class="game_title are1">
            <h3> flyboarding</h3>
            <p>

                <br> <b> Flyboarding</b> is an exciting and relatively new water sport that combines elements of jet
                skiing,
                snowboarding, and water propulsion. Here's some information about flyboarding:

                <br> <b> Equipment:</b> Flyboarding involves using a specialized water jet propulsion system attached to
                a board. The
                rider wears boots that are securely connected to the board by a hose, which is connected to a personal
                watercraft (PWC) or jet ski.

                <br><b> How it Works:</b> The rider stands on the flyboard, and water is forcefully pumped through the
                hose from the
                PWC. The high-pressure water jets beneath the board provide thrust, lifting the rider into the air and
                allowing for controlled flight.

                <br><b> Maneuverability:</b> Flyboard riders can perform a variety of maneuvers, including hovering in
                place, diving
                into the water, and soaring high above it. Skilled riders can execute tricks, spins, and flips.

                <br> <b> Safety:</b> Safety is a top priority in flyboarding. Riders wear life jackets and helmets, and
                instructors
                provide guidance on safe take-offs, landings, and control. The hose connecting the board to the PWC
                allows for easy disconnection in case of emergencies.

                <br> <b> Training:</b> Before attempting flyboarding, beginners typically receive instruction from
                certified
                instructors. Learning to balance and control the board takes practice, but many people can achieve basic
                flight in a short period.

                <br> <b>Locations:</b> Flyboarding is often offered at beach resorts, watersport centers, and coastal
                destinations
                worldwide. The availability of flyboarding may depend on local water regulations and weather conditions.

                <br> <b>Thrill Factor:</b> Flyboarding offers an adrenaline rush like no other. The sensation of
                hovering above the
                water's surface and performing aerial tricks is exhilarating.

                <br> <b> Accessibility:</b> While flyboarding may seem daunting, it's accessible to people of various
                skill levels,
                including beginners. Most participants find that with proper instruction and practice, they can quickly
                grasp the basics of the sport.

                <br> <b>Competitions:</b> Flyboarding has gained popularity as a competitive sport. Competitions
                showcase athletes
                performing intricate routines and stunts, and they are often judged on creativity and technical skill.

                <br> <b> Hydroflight Sports:</b> Flyboarding is part of a broader category of hydroflight sports, which
                also includes
                activities like jetpacking and hoverboarding. These sports all use water propulsion systems to achieve
                flight or controlled movement above the water.

                <br>Flyboarding is an exhilarating water sport that allows individuals to experience the thrill of
                flight
                above the water's surface. It's a unique and memorable activity that combines adventure, athleticism,
                and the joy of being propelled into the air by the power of water.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

